tldr; I designed the site with Claude and let Claude Code do its magic. Front-end development is just vibes now.

## How it started

Recently I discovered that Claude can make designs. I am well aware that Claude can write code (I use Claude Code at work), and that any LLM can make images, but a professional-looking, polished, coherent design? Guess I'll have to try it out myself.

## Previous design

The inspiration for the design was grid notebook paper. I wanted the website to feel like you are looking at a handwritten note on a grid notepad.

<PhotoRow>
  <Photo slug="old-home-light" />
  <Photo slug="old-home-dark" />
</PhotoRow>

I hand-coded this website initially: design, colours, styles, etc. I made a separate repository to share styles and components across my websites, this site included. There was only so much I could do myself in my spare time, and I wanted to keep the design simple anyway, so the overall design and colours looked basic.

<Photo slug="old-projects-dark" />

Looking back after the redesign, there were some aspects of this implementation I was not satisfied with. First, the dark theme didn't look right. The light theme was like handwriting on a white piece of paper, simple enough. However, flipping the colours does not magically make a good dark theme. Second, I am not good at drafting designs, and with little time to build and maintain it, the website ended up looking a bit flat and uninspiring. Finally, since I had already built a shared component library, I felt obligated to use it and keep my other websites consistent for consistency's sake, regardless of how they ended up looking.

## Vibe-design

I opened Claude on the web and prompted. Gave it the url to my live website and its github url. Described my inspiration and overall vibe I wanted, with some screenshots of notebooks and journals from Google. I specifically asked it to brainstorm ideas; I didn't need any code yet.

Its first response was long, verbose, and full of implementation detail. I skipped all the texts, asked for some mock designs instead and let it cook.

<Photo slug="first-mockups" />

Finally, more visuals, fewer words. I liked some designs, like the book cover version, or the preface page, but those didn't work well in mobile. The tabbed version gave a feel of paper on top of a desk, but also looked awkward on mobile. I then combined the elements I liked, mostly from journal landing, to make the current version of the website.

I had a theme for the look and feel in general: stationery. Background is dotted paper, easy enough. Ink colours for text and headings followed naturally. Not so with dark theme, dark coloured paper is uncommon after all. I went with film photography theme instead, and Claude borrowed colours from film emulsions.

Conveniently, all this happened in Claude on the web, I just chatted with Claude as I always did, and it made all the design as an artifact.

## Make it happen, Claude

Once I was happy with the design, I headed over to Claude Code on my desktop, gave it the artifact url, and let it do its magic. There was not many pages to begin with, within fifteen minutes I was looking at a dev server running the new design. I went to sleep at that point and left it there.

The next morning, I had a different idea. Instead of film photography theme, I went with chalkboard theme. Back to prompting Claude.

<Photo slug="chalkboard-home" />

Nice. Some adjustments later, I had a new dark theme. I played around with the new design a bit and asked for some animations. A couple of prompts later, light and dark theme is animated like flipping a page of a book.

<Photo slug="page-curl" />

## Old ways, new ways

A year ago, I would not have believed that AI could redesign my website. Made a version of it, sure, but not in the design and direction I wanted. Now it can. I can chat, upload images, point to some existing websites to brainstorm ideas, asked the model to create a mock design in the same chat, and use that design to create a working implementation.

Even better, I can keep the designs in Claude Artifacts as the master version, ask for changes, then ask Claude Code to make the changes. It also works the other way around: I could make the code changes first, then ask the model to update the design in the artifact. I don't need to maintain a Figma design or pay for a premium Figma seat, Claude has its own version of Figma now.

If you are looking to make your own website but don't know how to, you should give AI-assisted design and development a try. Remember: you still own the design. If you don't like what it makes, steer it to the direction you want.

Overall, I enjoyed this new process of designing my website, and I hope you like how it turned out.
